 "_about": "Hand-written. The 38 themes of the verse pool. For each: a display name, a one-line description, the short 'label' and neutral one-line 'blurb' a visitor sees when the topic is suggested or listed (and, for topics a sensitive message can be offered, a 'gentle_label' and 'gentle_blurb' shown instead), and modern words and phrases a visitor may use for it. Phrases are written as search/FORMAT.md step 1 splits text into words (lower case, \"can't\" -> \"can not\", \"I'm\" -> \"i\", no digits or apostrophes). A phrase is a list of slots separated by spaces; a slot may list alternatives with | and an alternative ending in * matches any word starting with it. Some themes have a short 'note' the site shows with their verses. Single words that often mean something else (passed, high, moved, fired, down, kid, lost) are only used inside phrases. Each theme's 'kind' (round 8) is 'comfort' or 'self_directed'. The comfort topics are grief, fear_anxiety, loneliness, illness, doubt_faith, hope and self_worth: every one of their verses was read and is comfort only, with no instruction to endure, wait, submit, keep silent, forgive or try harder (tests/test_search_suggest.py keeps a tripwire on directive words). Every other topic is self_directed: anger, forgiveness, guilt_repentance, temptation_self_control, patience, pride_humility, speech_gossip, work_effort, overwork_rest, suffering_endurance, peace_of_mind, envy_contentment, honesty, generosity, kindness_strangers, courage, love_compassion, friendship, marriage, family_parents, parenting, money_worry, purpose, gratitude, justice_wronged, guidance_decisions, failure, success, change_uncertainty, betrayal and wisdom_learning, whose verses ask the reader to change their own behaviour or to bear something. suggest() offers a self_directed topic only when the message names no other person, no event done to the writer, no mental-health wording and no anger at God (search/safety.json 'suggest' 'comfort_only_cues'; search/FORMAT.md step 8).",
